WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:06.740
Nerdfighters: Good morning John it's Friday!

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.030
Hank: I'm home from tour. I'm not well; I
acquired an illness! Ahhhuhuhhhhehhuhhh.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:14.910
I'm gonna take all the sniffles and coughs
from the recording of this video and put them

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.580
in right now...
[nose-blowing]

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.300
[SNOOOOORK]
[assorted other nose noises]

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.439
But just before I went on tour, I was doing
just fine. I was healthy and happy and I got

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:33.430
to visit Emily Graslie at the Chicago Field
Museum. Emily Graslie is the host of The Brain

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.470
Scoop. She and Michael Aranda created that
show here in Missoula, then it got acquired

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.700
by the Chicago Field Museum and she moved
there, which was sad, but it also means that

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.570
she can take me on a behind-the-scenes tour
of one of the biggest natural history museums

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.430
in the world! So not so bad!

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.309
Emily: This is a single tapeworm.
Hank: Yeah, that's what I thought it was.

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:53.220
Emily: That is wrapped around a metal plate.
If you have a tapeworm, and you try to get

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.080
rid of it and you break one of these segments
off -- all of the sexual reproductive organs

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.500
are located in the head, so it will continue
to reproduce, so you have to get the head

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.839
out.
Hank: Wouldn't it be weird if our reproductive

00:01:03.839 --> 00:01:05.450
organs were in our head?
Emily: Yeah.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.890
Hank: Oh! Wow!
E: It's a giant clam.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.390
H: That is a giant clam. We need to put a
banana next to it.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.590
E: A banana for scale?
H: It just looks... it just looks like a normal-sized

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:13.689
clam when I do this.

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:14.700
(Laughter)
H: Oh.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.719
E: That happens more than... yeah.

00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:19.409
E: Whoa! I've never seen these!
Other: (laughs)

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:21.090
H: H --- oooooooh!
E: Woooooooow!

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.820
H: Hi, big jar snakes.
E: That's a big jar snake.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:30.200
H: Now I can go in the vials. My hands are
getting really warm.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.859
E: Well, they -- they have to --
H: I expect they might be a pretty good insulator.

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:35.080
H: Like a real one?
E: Yeah, it's a real one! It's one of the

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:40.659
best specimens ever collected!
H: Like... oh man! I have a friend who's super

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:41.659
into dimetrodons.

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:42.851
Person: Yeah!
E: This is one of my favorite things -- this

00:01:42.851 --> 00:01:44.640
is a fossilized pine-cone.
H: What?!

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.939
E: All the organic material was replaced by
limestone. Couple hundred million years old.

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:52.659
No big deal. This is the last --
H: The only -- the only dodo skin?

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:56.229
E: -- specimen. They were actually this tiny
in real life. They made really good chicken

00:01:56.229 --> 00:01:57.710
nuggets, that's why they became extinct.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.549
H: Yeah! Bird heads!
E: Yeah. Why not?

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:03.869
E: This is the egg of an elephant bird. The
extinct elephant bird. It was, um, on the

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:07.069
island of Madagascar. To give you a sense
of scale -- I don't have any bananas, but

00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:10.679
this is an ostrich egg. And this is a real
one, a lot of museums have a replica, but

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:12.870
we have an actual one.
H: This is an actual egg, like if I dropped

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:17.531
that then it would be like, I, like I would
have to feel bad about that for the rest of

00:02:17.531 --> 00:02:18.550
my life.
E: Yeah, I -- I don't know, there's probably

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:22.190
less than half a dozen of these in any museum
collection in the world. Don't quote me on

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.750
that. I mean you could, you could put it in,
someone will tell me that I'm wrong.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.310
H: Could we take the banana for scale?
E: Yeah.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.102
H: Take it places.
E: Yeah, the banana for scale.

00:02:28.102 --> 00:02:30.394
H: Put it next to things.
E: This is the squirrel that I taxidermied

00:02:30.394 --> 00:02:31.394
recently.
H: Yeah! You made a squirrel! Yeah!

00:02:31.394 --> 00:02:34.000
E: Yeah, it's from our How to Taxidermy a
Squirrel episode!

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.560
E: And the guy who, who is the president of
the museum in Berlin, not only is he married

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.880
to the great-great-granddaughter of Charles
Darwin, he also has an amazing mustache that

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.850
is like this huge schwoopy thing, and I was
standing over there and the bar was here,

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:55.780
and he was standing in front of it, and his
mustache perfectly mimicked the horns of the

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.870
buffalo behind me. It was so perfect.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:02.190
H: So when Emily arrived at the Field Museum,
she found that there was one diorama space

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:07.600
in the Hall of African Mammals that just wasn't.
It was just plywood. In this wonderful, beautiful,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.610
gigantic natural history museum. And then,
for some reason, in the Reptile Room there

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:16.700
was a case full of African mammals. Hyenas.
So her first thought was "Why don't we put

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:22.250
those hyenas in a diorama in the African mammals
room? Cuz they're not reptiles."

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.050
Turns out that maybe the hyenas kinda got
ignored because they're not the most glamorous

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:30.280
of animals? But she thinks that's kinda unacceptable,
and I kinda do too, so they're doing an Indiegogo

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.130
campaign to place the hyenas in the diorama
in the Hall of African Mammals where they

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.550
belong. But it's expensive, so they are offering
all kinds of amazing perks.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:43.320
Tour was so fantastic, thank you to all the
people who came out to the shows, I hope that

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.810
you had as good of a time as I did, it seemed
like you did. John, I will see you... oh...

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.600
maybe before Tuesday? Oh?

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.910
I got this video of Emily holding a sign,
YouTube.com/brainscoop, so you could see the...

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:59.450
click on that and subscribe!

